  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Subtropical Storm Josephine located well off the Mid-\nAtlantic coast will move even farther away Thursday, however \nsome coastal impacts and hazardous marine conditions continue.\n\nSubtropical Storm Josephine located well off the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast will strengthen through tonight as it gradually turns more\nto the northeast. This northeast or east-northeast motion then \ncontinues on Thursday. Meanwhile, strong surface high pressure \ncentered across New England will shift into the Canadian \nMaritimes tonight. A tightened pressure gradient remains between\nthese two systems through tonight. \n\nSome impacts remain only for our coastal and marine areas. The multi-\nday north-northeast wind has resulted in 10+ foot waves just \noffshore. These big waves breaking onto the beaches will result in \nsome additional beach erosion especially during times of high tide. \nPeak wind gusts in the 30-40 mph range into this evening for areas \nalong the coast, and 20-30 mph inland. The winds will diminish late \nthis afternoon and especially tonight particularly away from the \nimmediate coast. Some mid to mostly high clouds into tonight will \noccur especially closer to the coast. As the winds diminish tonight, \nit will be a chilly night, however frost is not expected.\n\nFor Thursday into Friday, Subtropical Storm Josephine will move\nfarther out to sea as an incoming system from the northwest \nkicks it out. This will reduce the marine and coastal impacts, \nwhile keeping our weather dry and providing a fresh shot of \nchilly air. There will still be a notable north-northwest breeze\nThursday as the pressure gradient remains tightened due to \nanother incoming strong surface high from Canada. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tide cycles persist due to Subtropical \nStorm Josephine well of the coast with water levels peaking with \nthis evening's high tide. Widespread minor coastal flooding is \nexpected from Ocean County NJ southward through Delaware.\n\nAs Subtropical Storm Josephine lingers well off the coast elevated \ntide cycles persist with water levels peaking this evening. Expect \nfairly widespread but minor flooding from Ocean County NJ southward \nthrough Delaware including the Delaware shores of Delaware Bay. In \nsome of the back bays, moderate coastal flooding is still possible \nthis evening however the chances for this have lowered some as \nrecent tide cycles have generally not been quite as high as \nforecast. Beyond this evening, Josephine will gradually be \nmoving to the northeast away from the region for the end of the \nweek. Another round of minor coastal flooding is possible \nThursday evening though at this time it's looking to be more \nspotty in nature. \n\nAt this time we are not forecasting any coastal flooding for the \nMaryland eastern shore. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Frost impacts possible Friday night and Saturday \nnight where the growing season continues. \n\nAs an upper-level trough pivots across the Northeast on Friday,\na potentially strong mid level ridge in the Plains to Midwest \nshifts eastward some into the weekend. This will support strong \nsurface high pressure initially centered near the Great Lakes and \nportions of eastern Canada, which then shifts eastward into the \nNortheast and Mid-Atlantic Friday and over the weekend.\n\nA chilly and dry air mass will accompany this strong surface \nhigh, and as the high itself settles more overhead the winds \nwill decouple. This will set the stage for efficient radiational \ncooling both Friday night and Saturday night. The forecast lows are \nin the 30s for much of the area (away from the coasts and outside of \nthe urban centers) with a frost potential. It is possible that if \nthe radiational cooling ends up being stronger, then some areas \ncould get to freezing or a little below. Overall though, frost \nimpacts are possible where the growing season remains active.",
